1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,370
Here's our web app in two minutes: what it shows,

2
00:00:03,370 --> 00:00:06,970
how the data flows, and the rules your code has to follow.

3
00:00:06,970 --> 00:00:10,910
Fire satellites take a snapshot at about the same time every day.

4
00:00:10,910 --> 00:00:13,720
But NASA's Aqua satellite has drifted:

5
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:18,680
its 1:30 PM snapshot now happens near 3:50 PM over the Amazon,

6
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:20,750
and Terra is sliding earlier.

7
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:22,650
Fire activity changes through the day,

8
00:00:22,650 --> 00:00:25,580
so the counts shift even when the fires don't.

9
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:29,970
And Brazil's official fire record still uses Aqua as its reference.

10
00:00:29,970 --> 00:00:33,350
So VIIRS, which still looks at a fixed time, becomes our clock.

11
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:35,360
We learn the Aqua-to-VIIRS ratio

12
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:38,290
for each region and month from 2012 to 2020,

13
00:00:38,290 --> 00:00:39,470
before the drift.

14
00:00:39,470 --> 00:00:43,440
VIIRS times that ratio is a fixed-clock count, in Aqua units.

15
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:46,120
The gap between the real Aqua count and that number

16
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:49,600
shows how far an Aqua-based record has drifted.

17
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,000
That's the app: a fixed-clock fire calendar for anywhere.

18
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,870
Pick one of six preset regions.

19
00:00:54,870 --> 00:00:57,710
The calendar covers every month since 2002,

20
00:00:57,710 --> 00:01:02,080
colored by count or by anomaly against the historical median,

21
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:03,810
with a CSV download.

22
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:07,500
The audit panel shows that gap for every year since 2021,

23
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:08,610
with error bars.

24
00:01:08,610 --> 00:01:12,520
Next, the drift chart, and our centerpiece, the two-clocks test:

25
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:15,080
Terra and Aqua drift in opposite directions,

26
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:17,960
so comparing them isolates the clock effect.

27
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:20,300
Then California's detection limits,

28
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:22,210
checked against real recorded fires,

29
00:01:22,210 --> 00:01:24,850
and a “How do we know?” checklist.

30
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:25,800
Under the hood,

31
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:30,040
a Python pipeline turns NASA FIRMS hotspots into small JSON files,

32
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:32,200
one per panel, per region.

33
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:34,980
The app is static and reads only those files.

34
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:37,560
Until the real ones land in data/out,

35
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:41,720
build against data/mock: same layout, same fields.

36
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:42,740
Five rules.

37
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:45,130
Mock data always shows a MOCK badge.

38
00:01:45,130 --> 00:01:47,440
Null means no data, not zero.

39
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,970
Hatch provisional months and mark outages.

40
00:01:49,970 --> 00:01:53,460
Show “not enough fires to measure” instead of a noisy number.

41
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:56,600
And every number on screen comes from the JSON.

42
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,760
Start with the mock-data README. Let's build it.

